From ff75c09dc95f3538b2a5bb05d04bc3de437177c6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 16:05:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Timestamps: shift and byte-swap in unsigned types

ptp_write_binary_timestamps() and ptp_extract_timestamps() -- every
timestamp flexPTP sends or receives -- shift signed values into their sign
bit, which is undefined behaviour, in two places:

  - FLEXPTP_htonl() shifts its argument in the argument's own type, and
    both functions pass it TimestampI.nanosec, an int32_t. `(a) << 8`
    overflows for any nanosecond value of 2^23 or more, `(a) << 24` for
    any of 2^7 or more: nearly every timestamp.
  - The seconds are read by copying six bytes into the low end of
    TimestampI.sec, an int64_t, and then `ts->sec << 16`. On a little-
    endian target the last byte on the wire lands in the top byte, so
    the shift overflows whenever that byte is 0x80 or more: half of all
    received timestamps. Writing does the same shift, which overflows
    only for a negative or out-of-range seconds value.

GCC happens to produce the expected bytes, which is why nothing has ever
looked wrong on the wire. But the compiler is entitled not to, and UBSan
reports all three on the host:

  msg_utils.c:214:28: runtime error: left shift of 968000000 by 8
                      places cannot be represented in type 'int'
  msg_utils.c:245:42: runtime error: left shift of 246377198714880 by 16
                      places cannot be represented in type 'long int'
  msg_utils.c:246:23: runtime error: left shift of 216003845 by 8
                      places cannot be represented in type 'int'

FLEXPTP_htonl() now converts to uint32_t before it shifts, and the
seconds are shifted as uint64_t. Neither changes a value for any valid
timestamp: the htonl expression was already unsigned int (the 0xff000000
mask makes it so), its masks discard exactly the bits in which an
arithmetic and a logical right shift differ, and FLEXPTP_ntohll() and
FLEXPTP_htonll() take and return uint64_t anyway. The macro is still a
constant expression.

Not affected as used, so left alone: FLEXPTP_htons(), whose callers all
pass a uint16_t (0xffff << 8 fits in an int once promoted), and the
correctionField shift in the header, which is a uint64_t.

On the board it changes nothing but register allocation. Built with
arm-none-eabi-gcc 15.3 at -O2 for a Cortex-M33, every other function of
two firmware images is byte-identical, and in these two the only
difference is the operand order of one loop-exit `cmp`, which feeds a
`bne`. The nanosecond swap is a single `rev` before and after.

Found by running flexPTP's own engine on the host under ASan and UBSan.
A host test round-trips nanosecond values either side of 2^23 (0, 1,
127, 128, 2^23 - 1, 2^23, 968000000, 999999999) and a seconds value whose
last byte is 0xe0, and checks the exact wire bytes, both written and
read. Under UBSan it fails without this change and passes with it; in a
plain build it passes either way, as the firmware always has.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/msg_utils.c |  4 ++--
 src/flexptp/ptp_defs.h  | 12 +++++++-----
 2 files changed, 9 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c b/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
index 3419725..db26382 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
@@ -210,7 +210,7 @@ void ptp_write_binary_timestamps(void *pPayload, const TimestampI *ts, uint8_t n
     uint8_t i;
     for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
         // get timestamp data
-        uint64_t sec = FLEXPTP_htonll(ts->sec << 16);
+        uint64_t sec = FLEXPTP_htonll((uint64_t)ts->sec << 16);
         uint64_t nanosec = FLEXPTP_htonl(ts->nanosec);
 
         // fill in time data
@@ -242,7 +242,7 @@ void ptp_extract_timestamps(TimestampI *ts, void *pPayload, uint8_t n) {
         p += 4;
 
         // network->host
-        ts->sec = FLEXPTP_ntohll(ts->sec << 16);
+        ts->sec = FLEXPTP_ntohll((uint64_t)ts->sec << 16);
         ts->nanosec = FLEXPTP_ntohl(ts->nanosec);
 
         // step to next timestamp
diff --git a/src/flexptp/ptp_defs.h b/src/flexptp/ptp_defs.h
index 30e5521..c65117a 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/ptp_defs.h
+++ b/src/flexptp/ptp_defs.h
@@ -219,11 +219,13 @@ extern const uint8_t PTP_ETHERNET_PEER_DELAY[6]; ///< PTP's L2 Peer_Delay Ethern
 // ---- NETWORK-HOST CONVERSIONS ----
 // -- These are STATICALLY COMPUTABLE macros! ---
 
-#define FLEXPTP_htonl(a)          \
-    ((((a) >> 24) & 0x000000ff) | \
-     (((a) >> 8) & 0x0000ff00) |  \
-     (((a) << 8) & 0x00ff0000) |  \
-     (((a) << 24) & 0xff000000))
+// Shifted as uint32_t whatever the argument's type: TimestampI.nanosec is an int32_t, and shifting
+// a signed value into or past its sign bit is undefined.
+#define FLEXPTP_htonl(a)                      \
+    (((((uint32_t)(a)) >> 24) & 0x000000ff) | \
+     ((((uint32_t)(a)) >> 8) & 0x0000ff00) |  \
+     ((((uint32_t)(a)) << 8) & 0x00ff0000) |  \
+     ((((uint32_t)(a)) << 24) & 0xff000000))
 
 #define FLEXPTP_ntohl(a) FLEXPTP_htonl((a))
 
